#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/2614cbd879ea1014caf69d1239b69e81ebefcc76b8946962092cb83cc96dc987.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/149438cd70059230993ca93a2122158f71edb665b3948cbd4ff3a9c038d7e48f.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "217fe5bd-4ea4-57b9-8772-bb6e517207bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12416" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/6e97c59a7c736ec70142bc402566385fc2cd9c6aa78e304d70cefab1027078c1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\n- $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n\n- The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(0,4),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2,$ i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198161 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7127" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/4a20bc6cf78708f70fb1a696b9dc5971feef974df6429fa4169f82881c6d22e0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b719b318-d302-5052-9a9f-2c04a697bc02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324042} {:answer/id #uuid "262fba5d-ac5e-54bd-968b-1442aa1e7b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324043} {:answer/id #uuid "04c879d5-b9ae-5665-a4e5-f3829be5bafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=\\pm v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324044} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed34daf-85ff-56d1-89ed-98efc0952065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324045} {:answer/id #uuid "03228b9f-7eaa-5a7d-b545-1c7206e99a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm 2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262fba5d-ac5e-54bd-968b-1442aa1e7b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The only critical point is $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324043} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b35531-e501-5ca1-a4e9-fb38af098a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b086e929-2933-5923-87f3-a40760986e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-160460" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a85f0c9-440a-5e46-a1a3-ed53a4984c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324049} {:answer/id #uuid "55cb0a30-0412-5433-8065-e70b4846928c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324050} {:answer/id #uuid "9547cc08-919a-581e-a488-091512d8d9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324051} {:answer/id #uuid "84b7a5cc-cfbf-5eec-b766-bc9d8469fa1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324052} {:answer/id #uuid "2e87d794-a38b-5848-84d8-7fd5ed580eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e87d794-a38b-5848-84d8-7fd5ed580eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324053} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f676447-132c-5db6-8347-2b9a917c9ceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194e4843-dc33-5afd-bdc7-3ac1c1ef2ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324056} {:answer/id #uuid "b2687845-7270-5e6b-bf80-18af3c3faf92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=-2v$ and $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324057} {:answer/id #uuid "0c35f47a-8ebc-57fa-9c24-5e64620a4a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the hyperbola ${v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324058} {:answer/id #uuid "46631a05-f41c-598d-8043-806b1f53599c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324059} {:answer/id #uuid "07e59a61-c9e7-5b88-94a0-151cf98656dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "194e4843-dc33-5afd-bdc7-3ac1c1ef2ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0$ and on the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324056} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b94ee39-c4fb-5827-a1f0-aa4a945374d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f0fb416-ba54-5520-a3cd-23b891dc09c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-160477" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=u{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+2v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv \\\\ 2u & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{2}\\cdot 2-2uv\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{v}^{2}-4{u}^{2}v & =0 \\\\ v(2v-4{u}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v(2v-4{u}^{2})=0$ defines the line $v=0,$ i.e., the $u$-axis, and the parabola $v=2{u}^{2}$ in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a801c113-56c8-5b89-82db-87c58217ea10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324063} {:answer/id #uuid "676eba50-edb9-56af-bdad-5b0d436ae887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324064} {:answer/id #uuid "c94f8e40-fc3c-598a-b89d-862c2821fdce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324065} {:answer/id #uuid "489d6705-792c-5948-bc51-d0f64ec6adf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324066} {:answer/id #uuid "1466b790-196d-5e83-b022-527990c5f9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1466b790-196d-5e83-b022-527990c5f9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324067} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf47f4f6-db5d-5d60-94e6-b528e52c0e24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec613c2-8b31-57c1-8b1d-7f35687a5b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-160563" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84194ed3-152e-51ef-9fed-4d26e5b97dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324070} {:answer/id #uuid "d85a72eb-8030-543e-884a-73d01b31b02f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324071} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2f2ebf-ed3f-5d89-be74-95d5dbc33054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324072} {:answer/id #uuid "96bc3664-62f5-58c7-9359-a5e0bddaf40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324073} {:answer/id #uuid "9c59a974-ba57-5f3c-af4c-5070b0609f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84194ed3-152e-51ef-9fed-4d26e5b97dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324070} :answer-field/id #uuid "2530e453-37b5-5e8b-a1d1-14494d277462" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e6a6139-c56b-5ec6-8b4e-5f56e91ad2e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160581" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x+7y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=-x+7y$ and $v(x,y)=3x-y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae1a655-99be-5655-a3be-505d223f0a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324077} {:answer/id #uuid "042f64df-fc71-5aeb-870e-58020f56f1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324078} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffd7393-b068-5965-ab30-48cd53a98bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324079} {:answer/id #uuid "fd173bfa-2606-5ec8-a9a8-f67b2d42062f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324080} {:answer/id #uuid "6223297b-3f1f-5765-a0be-fad3e5b45c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae1a655-99be-5655-a3be-505d223f0a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324077} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97d62fd-b53d-5eea-a97f-b50e17a3172a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1936251c-b682-5e21-bc78-b5e80d9892c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161845" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c04578-6691-5740-b87f-bfc4e34d68b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324084} {:answer/id #uuid "8eece770-d44b-5537-bfbf-470cb187e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324085} {:answer/id #uuid "82038214-af23-5d92-b605-c479872b7f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324086} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb2816b-bc01-5a73-b318-d64cab1bbbbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324087} {:answer/id #uuid "38d8610e-2763-5eab-b757-cdf505e84275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eece770-d44b-5537-bfbf-470cb187e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324085} :answer-field/id #uuid "655e28ae-f320-5522-a2dd-7a0b4bf9b7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bb25d39-9aff-5295-b9a5-be77270cb4ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2570335-64e0-5b4e-82ce-b8e5464c0e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324091} {:answer/id #uuid "0920a172-bebe-563b-8068-37ef6b1c26a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324092} {:answer/id #uuid "89121b65-a753-58fb-bee5-239bc2c009a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324093} {:answer/id #uuid "8821660d-3b41-5a3c-abfa-c01dd286b01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} {:answer/id #uuid "f13b29a6-0267-5e68-a25e-d6816fae062e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8821660d-3b41-5a3c-abfa-c01dd286b01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} :answer-field/id #uuid "10cb6b95-c8b6-5ea3-a13a-c33055d49cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f944412-33cb-5081-bdb5-c7764378e69c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161858" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/540e1fac13ed45d6db5c857678a7ccf410b3834213adb2b984cb6f942d4dba2f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=2=f(-2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-2$ since ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=4=f(0),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=0.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=0,$ for example, $(-0.1,0.1),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=0,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(0)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee12a02-d879-59da-aec7-ea2d6571bb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324098} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac17f11-f929-58fa-8b29-6c363aa3e029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324099} {:answer/id #uuid "7e404ff8-d9cf-50ab-89d9-e6193a39ec62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324100} {:answer/id #uuid "e07f5743-1c94-583c-bc78-5c2b929a6aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324101} {:answer/id #uuid "e2535b4e-f3e6-55d4-b086-efea0bad6828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e404ff8-d9cf-50ab-89d9-e6193a39ec62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324100} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa57363-df1b-58f2-837f-51d51b554ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d66e1b1-7b49-597f-a208-7098855dadc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161865" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0.5,1.5),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-0.88=f(0),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is also false. Notice that $y=2.5=f(3).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=3$ since ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9c3e1ed-6dca-51d3-ad1c-1d7def1f515d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324105} {:answer/id #uuid "f26047d6-64fd-55d0-a8ff-9b0d3f1f2a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324106} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec442b8-94e4-56aa-8807-ad0fa1f1a88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324107} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7d93b-cf32-51b8-80b9-52d26cf641a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324108} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ecc69b-0208-57ba-9d44-59089af5da16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ec442b8-94e4-56aa-8807-ad0fa1f1a88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d52f285-c0db-5927-ad1c-6a75afccabc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0bc89ed-3046-579a-96f4-412ae4e827ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-161866" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044887/assets/675226780bb9627524f4ab03691e84e312300f398f352abc3b698c1fcdd83490.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-1$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=0$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=1$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=1=f(-1).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1$ since ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=0=f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(1,3),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=2,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(2)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1640e8b4-b2ab-5e2f-91cb-8ef692109cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324111} {:answer/id #uuid "e80b04c9-a1ac-577a-811d-8ce910e2fd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324112} {:answer/id #uuid "eb844b1f-c4e4-5fd2-8cf2-d8b2ea7f2da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324113} {:answer/id #uuid "66cd5471-a3a9-5b0f-bd01-a85a8ddea804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324114} {:answer/id #uuid "052efdc3-ad52-5311-be2c-b97b25e083a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb844b1f-c4e4-5fd2-8cf2-d8b2ea7f2da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324113} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7191d8e-2bb6-54be-9067-def21eae756d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a6af403-e6c8-5c17-8e90-e6cdab8d3462" :question/math-academy-id "q-81169" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=uv$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ v & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "557ad530-b79f-5d43-9d3b-3beaa66d4fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324118} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a795de-9ba5-5977-8acf-f0c059ac74ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324119} {:answer/id #uuid "2d554aa2-a151-5249-9902-b06b8467a6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324120} {:answer/id #uuid "b623dfb1-f71e-588f-b887-aec5ef8b7d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324121} {:answer/id #uuid "56551ca8-13bc-5452-848d-039a8c0d943c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $uv=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b623dfb1-f71e-588f-b887-aec5ef8b7d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324121} :answer-field/id #uuid "4030c9c3-32ab-5c33-9366-129b0d91d1e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08b26d40-c5f1-558a-a1d7-4ddf15b255a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81274" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{uv}$ and $y(u,v)=u+2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v{e}^{uv} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u{e}^{uv} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot 2-u{e}^{uv}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(2v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{uv}(2v-u) & =0 \\\\ 2v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe equation $u=2v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b387d35-4fed-517c-8ba8-94b95e204b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197810} {:answer/id #uuid "a24b045e-7cf0-503f-be5a-8ba593a2c54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197811} {:answer/id #uuid "cc13729e-a24d-5dd5-b9f5-e0d9e96164bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197812} {:answer/id #uuid "20e4ae68-df4f-5dff-8476-07d9ce71f8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197813} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcff66d-5fe2-5e46-b1a7-c1f159cd365e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc13729e-a24d-5dd5-b9f5-e0d9e96164bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197812} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf356fa4-81bb-57dc-9099-343ca13a886b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8fc056f-3ac3-54ec-bc57-4220e1dc20f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324125} {:answer/id #uuid "19d6a6a6-23cc-569c-8631-f2e11b441797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324126} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d5a325-cc43-542a-8a42-5a21e54e484e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324127} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf4fc49-b901-5679-953b-d2c3801fe6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324128} {:answer/id #uuid "8c69e388-b6fb-5e1d-8ef8-8a8c8738942b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cf4fc49-b901-5679-953b-d2c3801fe6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324128} :answer-field/id #uuid "346a1dc7-6e51-5672-8084-22f514684aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cea4382-2466-585e-b610-d8ae9b9bac0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81333" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+2y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5150d7-1e56-52ae-8b81-8b7fbcda71d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324132} {:answer/id #uuid "5af6ec9d-299c-5fef-9cc2-8cd4d46b28c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324133} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f5ebb5-db7f-5b89-af73-e842139f0470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} {:answer/id #uuid "dc69252c-a54e-5d0d-9fa1-5c5c9e8dea28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324135} {:answer/id #uuid "8a05c7ef-16f1-5d39-b61c-b653c9107f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5af6ec9d-299c-5fef-9cc2-8cd4d46b28c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324133} :answer-field/id #uuid "01676cf1-bde2-5293-a4a0-fcec2cf155a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49fc0404-bc55-50c7-a26f-a9bc9a4c5efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-81370" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-4y \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=5x-4y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =5 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
